<p>Normal private equity buys a company, holds it for 3–7 years, and exits. HUB's majority owner <strong>Hellman &amp; Friedman has held for 13 years and counting</strong>. Rather than selling outright, H&amp;F has done four successive minority sales — letting employees and earlier sponsors take liquidity while H&amp;F retains control.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Event</th><th>Valuation</th><th>Revenue</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>2007</td><td>Apax + Morgan Stanley take HUB private</td><td>~$1.8B</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>2013</td><td>Hellman &amp; Friedman acquires</td><td>$4.4B</td><td>$1.1B</td></tr>
<tr><td>2018</td><td>Altas Partners minority stake</td><td>$10.0B</td><td>$2.0B+</td></tr>
<tr><td>2023</td><td>Leonard Green &amp; Partners minority stake</td><td>$23.0B</td><td>$3.7B</td></tr>
<tr><td>May 2025</td><td>T. Rowe Price / Alpha Wave / Temasek round</td><td><strong>$29.0B</strong></td><td>$4.77B (2024)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The 2023 deal introduced a <strong>Liquid Private Placement (LPP)</strong> — a recurring liquidity window that lets existing shareholders periodically sell shares without forcing a full company sale. About 30% of HUB equity is historically held by employees, so the LPP is the mechanism by which producers and management actually realize value.</p>
<p>The 2025 round is the <strong>most consequential structural move for HSO to understand</strong>: it brought in mutual funds, hedge funds, and sovereign wealth funds — public-market-style investors. All equity raised was <strong>common stock</strong>, no preferred, no convertibles. The cap table now looks pre-public. CEO Marc Cohen ran ~100 investor meetings over 15 months to get there.</p>
"""),
