"03-rpw": [
    ("One sentence",
     "What RPW is",
     """
<p>RPW is HUB's combined <strong>retirement-plan-advisor (institutional) and registered-investment-advisor (retail wealth) practice</strong>, with about <strong>$178 billion in client assets</strong> as of mid-2025, built largely through 25-plus strategic acquisitions since 2019 and growing aggressively.</p>
<p>This is the practice that doesn't look like insurance brokerage. Understanding it is how you understand 30–40% of where HUB's strategic energy goes, and it's the part of HUB's business where the ERP Unite data model has the most to prove.</p>
"""),
    ("The two client books",
     "Institutional plan sponsor vs. private wealth",
     """
<p>RPW serves two distinct businesses, often through the same regional offices but with different teams, fee models, and regulatory frameworks:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Book A — Institutional</th><th>Book B — Private Wealth</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Client</strong></td><td>The employer sponsoring the retirement plan (company, hospital, school district, nonprofit)</td><td>The individual — typically an executive, business owner, or HNW family</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>HUB's role</strong></td><td>Fiduciary investment advisor under ERISA</td><td>Personal financial advisor under SEC RIA rules</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Fee level</strong></td><td>0.05%–0.50% of plan assets (large plans); 0.50%–1.00%+ (small plans). Also flat fees and per-participant fees.</td><td>0.50%–1.00% of AUM</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Volume vs. margin</strong></td><td>Higher volume, lower margin</td><td>Lower volume, higher margin</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Data source</strong></td><td>Recordkeeper feeds (Fidelity, Empower, Voya, Principal, etc.)</td><td>Custodian feeds (Schwab, Fidelity Institutional, Pershing)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The two books feed each other constantly. The CFO who hires HUB to advise her company's 401(k) often becomes a private wealth client. Retiring participants from a plan-sponsor client often roll their balances into HUB-managed IRAs — and that rollover is <strong>the single most valuable moment in the customer lifecycle</strong>, because the same dollars that were paying institutional fees (~25 bps) now pay retail wealth fees (~75–100 bps).</p>
"""),
    ("Plan sponsor work",
     "What 'fiduciary investment advisor' actually produces",
     """
<p>When HUB is hired by a plan sponsor, the deliverables look like this:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Investment Policy Statement (IPS)</strong> — written document spelling out how the plan picks and monitors investments. HUB drafts it.</li>
<li><strong>Investment menu design</strong> — choosing the fund lineup participants can pick from, including the <strong>QDIA</strong> (Qualified Default Investment Alternative — what a participant gets if they don't actively choose, almost always a target-date fund family today).</li>
<li><strong>Fund monitoring</strong> — quarterly performance, risk, and expense reports; documenting fund replacement decisions.</li>
<li><strong>Fee benchmarking</strong> — comparing what the plan pays the recordkeeper, advisor, and funds against peer plans. Recommended annually.</li>
<li><strong>Vendor RFPs</strong> — running formal search processes when the plan evaluates switching recordkeepers. Recommended every 3–5 years.</li>
<li><strong>Fiduciary committee training</strong> — quarterly or annual sessions teaching the plan sponsor's HR/Finance team their ERISA duties.</li>
<li><strong>Plan design consulting</strong> — Roth? Auto-enroll? Auto-escalate? Match true-up? Profit sharing? NQDC for the top 50?</li>
<li><strong>Participant education and financial wellness</strong> — group meetings, webinars, 1-on-1 sessions for employees.</li>
<li><strong>Compliance support</strong> — Form 5500 review, SECURE 2.0 amendment tracking, audit support.</li>
<li><strong>Litigation defense support</strong> — if the plan is sued for excessive fees.</li>
</ul>
"""),
    ("The five plan types",
     "Critical to learn — these come up in scope conversations",
     """
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Plan type</th><th>Who can offer it</th><th>Key features</th><th>2026 deferral limit</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>401(k)</strong></td><td>Private sector employers</td><td>Most common DC plan; Roth + pre-tax + after-tax; matched contributions; ERISA-governed</td><td>$23,500 (+ catch-up)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>403(b)</strong></td><td>Public schools, 501(c)(3) nonprofits, religious orgs</td><td>Similar to 401(k); historically annuity-dominated, now mostly mutual funds; some are ERISA-exempt</td><td>$23,500 (+ catch-up)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>457(b)</strong></td><td>Governmental units; certain tax-exempt orgs (top-hat)</td><td>Deferred comp plan; <strong>no 10% early-withdrawal penalty</strong> after separation; can be stacked with a 401(k) or 403(b); special 3-year catch-up before retirement</td><td>$23,500 — separate from 401(k)/403(b)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Defined Benefit (pension)</strong></td><td>Private and public sector</td><td>Traditional pension; employer bears investment risk; actuarial valuation required; increasingly closed/frozen</td><td>N/A (benefit-formula based)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>NQDC</strong></td><td>Top executives only (top-hat plans exempt from most ERISA rules)</td><td>For highly compensated executives above qualified plan limits; unfunded promise to pay</td><td>No statutory limit</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Why this matters in practice: a school district has a 403(b) + a 457(b) — RPW handles both, often with different vendors. A tech company has a 401(k) for everyone + an NQDC for the top 50 — RPW handles both, plus the wealth side for those executives. TCG (acquired by HUB in 2021) was a Texas firm specializing in 403(b)/457(b) for public-sector plans; that acquisition was specifically how HUB entered the public-sector retirement market.</p>
"""),
    ("Fiduciary roles",
     "3(21) vs. 3(38) — memorize this distinction",
     """
<p>ERISA defines two paid fiduciary roles a retirement plan can hire. The difference comes up in <em>every</em> RPW conversation.</p>
<div class="callout callout-split">
<div>
<p><strong>ERISA §3(21) — "Investment Advice Fiduciary"</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Advisor <em>recommends</em> investments and decisions.</li>
<li>The plan sponsor's fiduciary committee makes the final decision and bears the legal liability.</li>
<li>Lower fee, more common, less risk transfer.</li>
<li>The HR/Finance committee still has to vote on every fund change.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div>
<p><strong>ERISA §3(38) — "Investment Manager"</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Advisor has <strong>discretionary authority</strong> to select, monitor, and replace investments without sponsor approval.</li>
<li>The plan sponsor delegates the fiduciary liability for those investment decisions to the 3(38).</li>
<li>Higher fee, less common, more risk transfer.</li>
<li>The sponsor just monitors the advisor — the advisor monitors the funds.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<p>HUB RPW offers both. Larger plans increasingly prefer 3(38) because it offloads litigation risk. The industry trend is toward more 3(38) engagements. There's a third role worth knowing — <strong>§3(16) plan administrator</strong> — which is administrative (Form 5500 filing, distributions, etc.) rather than investment-related. HUB doesn't usually take this; it's typically handled by a TPA.</p>
"""),
    ("The legal entity quilt",
     "Why RPW operates as a federation",
     """
<p>When HUB buys an RPA firm, it doesn't re-paper all the client accounts overnight. So RPW operates as a <strong>federation of subsidiaries</strong>, each with its own SEC RIA registration or broker-dealer affiliation:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Global Retirement Partners, LLC (GRP)</strong> — SEC RIA. Was LPL Financial's largest retirement-focused branch. Acquired via the wave of GRP-affiliate purchases starting 2019.</li>
<li><strong>Sheridan Road Advisors, LLC</strong> — SEC RIA. From the 2019 Sheridan Road acquisition.</li>
<li><strong>TCG Advisors</strong> — SEC RIA. From the 2021 Trusted Capital Group acquisition (Texas; strong in 403(b)/457(b)).</li>
<li><strong>HUB International Investment Advisory Services, Inc.</strong> — SEC RIA. HUB's house RIA.</li>
<li><strong>HUB Investment Advisors, LLC</strong> — SEC RIA.</li>
<li><strong>Sheridan Road / Millennium / Perennial</strong> — regional sub-brands with their own client lists.</li>
<li><strong>HUB International Investment Services, Inc.</strong> — FINRA broker-dealer (member FINRA/SIPC).</li>
<li>Plus partnerships with <strong>outside broker-dealers</strong>, notably <strong>LPL Financial</strong> (many ex-GRP advisors retain LPL affiliations).</li>
</ul>
<p>A single HUB advisor can switch which entity an account sits under depending on what's best for the client — RIA vs. commission, ERISA fiduciary vs. not, fee model. From the data perspective this means <strong>multi-entity reporting</strong> on the wealth side is non-trivial: the same advisor may have books across 4–5 distinct legal entities, each with its own custodian feed and regulatory disclosure obligations.</p>
"""),
    ("AUM growth",
     "The acquisition flywheel, in numbers",
     """
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>AUM</th><th>Driver</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Feb 2021</td><td>$93B</td><td>Baseline at DeNoyior appointment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dec 2023</td><td>$148B</td><td>Post-AFS acquisition</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nov 2024</td><td>$148B+</td><td>Post-CSi acquisition ($1.1B added)</td></tr>
<tr><td>May 2025</td><td>$178B</td><td>Post-Prestige Wealth Partners</td></tr>
<tr><td>June 2025</td><td>$178B+</td><td>Post-Veripax Wealth Management</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Doubled in four years. Most of that is acquisition, not market appreciation. RPW is in the active growth-by-M&amp;A phase, and the M&amp;A advisor of choice (<strong>Wise Rhino Group</strong>) appears on most of these deals — Peter Campagna and Dick Darian are the named partners on the seller side.</p>
"""),
    ("HSO implication",
     "How ERP Unite handles RPW — and what's different at HUB's scale",
     """
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-eyebrow">The OneDigital proof point</div>
<p>OneDigital runs both its insurance brokerage and its Retirement &amp; Wealth practice on ERP Unite today. The same statement-ingest → cash app → AH journal → accrual pipeline carries advisory-fee revenue alongside commission revenue. <code>CompensationLine</code>'s <code>RevenueType</code>, <code>ProductType</code>, <code>ServicePeriod</code>, and flexible <code>Amount</code> fields are the existing extension points — no new schema needed for the core revenue capture.</p>
<div class="callout-eyebrow" style="margin-top:18px;">What HUB-specific work actually looks like</div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Recordkeeper + custodian feed onboarding.</strong> Fidelity, Empower, Vanguard/Ascensus, Voya, T. Rowe Price, Principal, Transamerica, Schwab Retirement Plan Services on the recordkeeper side; Schwab Institutional, Fidelity Institutional, Pershing on the wealth custodian side. Each is a statement format that Mapping Studio onboards the same way it onboards a carrier statement — bring an example file, let Gemini propose the field mappings, human review approves. <strong>The harder lift is the breadth of formats, not the integration mechanism.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Fiduciary engagement type tagging.</strong> 3(21) / 3(38) / 3(16) classifications flow through compensation reporting for regulatory disclosure. Configuration — adds a dimension to existing reporting; doesn't change the line model.</li>
<li><strong>Plan and participant entity modeling.</strong> The "client" can be the plan (institutional) or the participant (post-rollover wealth) — and the same person can be both. Customer/contact modeling extension; matches how OneDigital handles their own R&amp;W client book.</li>
<li><strong>Multi-entity producer comp.</strong> An advisor's payouts span RIA, BD, and outside-BD entities. CompHub's contract framework already supports this — the implementation work is rule authoring per advisor cohort, not platform change.</li>
<li><strong>Regulatory disclosure outputs.</strong> Form ADV, 408(b)(2), Form 5500 are reporting layers built on the same line-level data. Additive reporting work — not foundational.</li>
<li><strong>Acquired-entity normalization at scale.</strong> 600+ acquired brands on the broker side and ~25 RPA firms on the RPW side push IntegrationHub's canonical-entity normalization further than any prior deployment. <strong>This is the real engineering effort.</strong></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Net:</strong> RPW at HUB is a reuse-and-extend engagement, not a product-build engagement. The deck for discovery should lead with "OneDigital does this today" and frame the work as <em>statement-template onboarding + configuration + reporting layers at unprecedented scale</em>.</p>
</div>
"""),
