"04-rpa-industry": [
    ("Size of the prize",
     "The numbers that frame everything else",
     """
<p>U.S. defined contribution plans (401(k)s, 403(b)s, 457(b)s) hold roughly <strong>$10+ trillion</strong> in assets. About 70 million Americans participate. Every dollar of that pool generates fees somewhere along the chain — recordkeeper revenue, advisor revenue, fund-manager revenue, custodial revenue. RPA aggregators have grown collectively from <strong>~$1 trillion in 2018</strong> to <strong>$2.6+ trillion in 2024</strong> in DC assets under advisement.</p>
"""),
    ("Three-layer cake",
     "Recordkeeper vs. advisor vs. participant — keep these straight",
     """
<p>Every retirement plan involves three layers that constantly get confused. Keep them clean.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Who</th><th>What they do</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>1 — Recordkeeper</strong></td><td>Technology + operations company</td><td>Tracks every participant's balance, processes contributions, handles loans/withdrawals, sends statements, files Form 5500, runs the website and call center</td><td>Fidelity, Empower, Vanguard/Ascensus, Voya, T. Rowe Price, Principal, Transamerica, Schwab Retirement Plan Services</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>2 — Advisor (RPA)</strong></td><td>The fiduciary intermediary</td><td>Sits between sponsor and recordkeeper. Recommends investments, advises on vendor selection, provides fiduciary advice, runs employee education, monitors fund performance. Doesn't hold money.</td><td>Captrust, Aon/NFP, <strong>HUB RPW</strong>, OneDigital, SageView, Mercer, WTW</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>3 — Participant</strong></td><td>The actual employee</td><td>Owns the money. Interacts with the recordkeeper for transactions. Sometimes interacts with the advisor for education and personal advice. Rollover IRAs at job change drive advisor wealth-management revenue.</td><td>The 70M people in DC plans</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>HUB <strong>does not compete with Fidelity, Empower, or Vanguard</strong>. HUB competes with Captrust, NFP (now Aon), OneDigital, SageView, Mercer, and WTW. HUB <strong>partners with</strong> the recordkeepers — it might place a client's plan on Fidelity's platform while remaining the advisor. Misreading this in a customer conversation is the fastest way to look uninformed.</p>
"""),
    ("The RPA aggregator phenomenon",
     "Why the cottage industry collapsed into roll-ups",
     """
<p>Until about 2015, the RPA business was a fragmented cottage industry — thousands of small firms, often a few advisors plus support staff, serving local employers. Two trends collapsed the fragmentation:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>ERISA fee litigation got expensive.</strong> Small RPA firms couldn't afford the E&amp;O insurance, compliance staff, or technology to defend themselves against the class-action wave.</li>
<li><strong>Plan sponsors started demanding more.</strong> Financial wellness, advanced analytics, participant-level advice, ESG screening, lifetime income solutions, AI-driven plan benchmarking. Small firms couldn't keep up.</li>
</ol>
<p>PE-backed aggregators emerged that could buy small RPAs, give them scale, and roll up the industry.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Aggregator</th><th>Backing</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Captrust</strong></td><td>GTCR + Carlyle</td><td>~$1T+ in client assets. RPA + RIA hybrid. Largest.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Aon (via NFP)</strong></td><td>Public</td><td>Aon acquired NFP for $13.4B in April 2024 — instantly a top-tier mid-market RPA + benefits aggregator.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>HUB RPW</strong></td><td>H&amp;F + Altas + LGP + 2025 round</td><td>~$178B AUM. Insurance-broker-plus-RPA model.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>OneDigital Retirement + Wealth</strong></td><td>PE-backed</td><td>Same retirement-plus-benefits play as HUB. Notable for HSO because OneDigital is the source of ERP Unite.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>SageView Advisory Group</strong></td><td>PE-backed</td><td>Institutional consulting.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Mercer (Marsh McLennan)</strong></td><td>Public</td><td>Institutional retirement consulting; large-plan focus.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>WTW Investments</strong></td><td>Public</td><td>Large-plan delegated solutions (OCIO).</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Mariner Wealth Advisors</strong></td><td>PE-backed</td><td>Crossover RIA aggregator that also does retirement plans.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
"""),
    ("Consolidation curve",
     "Fred Barstein's framework — where each layer is right now",
     """
<p>Industry analyst Fred Barstein (founder of the Retirement Adviser University) maps the industry on Harvard Business Review's "Consolidation Curve":</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stage 1 — Opening.</strong> Fragmented industry with hundreds of small players. <em>RPA industry pre-2015.</em></li>
<li><strong>Stage 2 — Scale.</strong> Roll-ups and aggregators consolidate. <strong>RPA industry is here right now</strong> — HUB is one of the consolidators.</li>
<li><strong>Stage 3 — Focus.</strong> Top 5–10 players control most market share. <strong>Recordkeepers are here</strong> — the Fab Five (Fidelity, Empower, Vanguard's successor, Voya, Principal) hold ~78% of assets.</li>
<li><strong>Stage 4 — Balance &amp; Alliance.</strong> Mature, low-growth, alliance-driven competition.</li>
</ul>
<p>The mental model: <strong>RPAs are about 5 years behind recordkeepers on the consolidation curve.</strong> Expect another decade of aggregator M&amp;A before things settle.</p>
"""),
    ("Convergence",
     "The strategic concept that explains HUB's acquisition path",
     """
<p>The most important strategic concept in the current RPA industry: <strong>convergence</strong>. The thesis is that retirement plan advisory, wealth management, and employee benefits are converging into a single client offering.</p>
<p>Why:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Participant demand.</strong> Workers want help with their <em>whole</em> financial life — student debt, emergency savings, HSAs, rollovers, retirement income, taxes — not just picking 401(k) funds.</li>
<li><strong>Plan sponsor demand.</strong> Employers want a single trusted partner handling 401(k) + health plan + executive benefits + financial wellness.</li>
<li><strong>Litigation defense.</strong> A plan sponsor that can show comprehensive participant advice has stronger fiduciary defense.</li>
<li><strong>Economics.</strong> Capturing the rollover when a participant leaves is enormously valuable — wealth-management revenue at 75–100 bps versus institutional RPA fees at 25–50 bps.</li>
</ul>
<p>HUB is positioned exactly for this convergence. It owns retirement + wealth + benefits + insurance under one roof. So do NFP (now Aon), OneDigital, and Captrust. Firms that only do one of the three are at a structural disadvantage and are typical acquisition targets.</p>
"""),
    ("How money flows",
     "RPA business model — fee mix in detail",
     """
<ul>
<li><strong>Asset-based fees (most common).</strong> Typically 0.05%–0.50% of plan assets for large plans, 0.50%–1.00%+ for smaller plans. HUB's larger plans negotiate sliding-scale rates that decline as assets grow.</li>
<li><strong>Flat fees.</strong> Increasingly common for fiduciary consulting, especially jumbo plans where AUM-based fees would be exorbitant.</li>
<li><strong>Per-participant fees.</strong> Often layered alongside asset-based fees.</li>
<li><strong>3(38) premium.</strong> Discretionary management commands materially higher fees than non-discretionary 3(21) advice.</li>
<li><strong>Cross-sell to wealth.</strong> Highest-margin path. A participant retires with $500K in their 401(k), rolls it to an IRA managed by HUB at 0.75–1.00% — 5–10× revenue uplift versus institutional plan advisory.</li>
</ul>
<p>Rule of thumb: institutional plan-advisory revenue is roughly 25–50 bps of assets per year; retail wealth management on those same assets after rollover is 75–100 bps. The whole industry's strategic obsession is <strong>capturing the rollover</strong>.</p>
"""),
    ("RFP and benchmarking cycle",
     "How new RPA business is actually won",
     """
<p>Most retirement plans rebid the advisor and/or recordkeeper periodically:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Recordkeeper RFP</strong> — every 3–5 years is best practice; some plans wait longer.</li>
<li><strong>Advisor RFP</strong> — every 5–7 years is increasingly seen as best practice (industry polls put this at 81% support).</li>
<li><strong>Fee benchmarking</strong> — annually for most plans now.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is how HUB wins new business: incumbent advisor's contract comes up for review, HUB participates in the RFP, demonstrates better fees/services/technology/specialization, wins the plan. The process usually involves an independent search consultant — Curcio Webb, North Pier Consulting, InHub, Wagner Law Group — hired by the plan sponsor.</p>
<p>Key phrase to internalize: <strong>"reasonable fees."</strong> ERISA requires plan fees to be <em>reasonable</em> — not cheapest. Reasonable means appropriate relative to services delivered. A high fee with extensive services is defensible; a low fee with no services can be a fiduciary problem if the plan needs more.</p>
"""),
    ("Test yourself",
     "Five questions before moving on",
     """
<ol class="test-yourself">
<li>Distinguish recordkeeper, advisor, and participant. Where does HUB sit?</li>
<li>What stage of the consolidation curve are recordkeepers in? RPAs?</li>
<li>What is "convergence" and which HUB acquisition decisions does it explain?</li>
<li>Why is a participant's <em>rollover</em> the most valuable moment in the customer lifecycle for an RPA?</li>
<li>How often should a plan sponsor RFP (a) its recordkeeper and (b) its advisor?</li>
</ol>
"""),
],

"05-regulatory": [
    ("Why this module matters",
     "ERISA is the law underneath everything HUB RPW does",
     """
<p>The entire retirement-plan business exists inside one law (<strong>ERISA</strong>) and is shaped by a series of follow-on laws (the <strong>SECURE Acts</strong>) and ongoing <strong>DOL rulemaking</strong>. You can't talk about HUB's RPW practice intelligently without knowing this material. It also explains why HUB has the customers it has: <strong>ERISA litigation</strong> is the single biggest driver of demand for fiduciary advisory services.</p>
"""),
    ("ERISA (1974)",
     "The foundation",
     """
<p>The <strong>Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974</strong> is the federal law that governs private-sector retirement plans (and most welfare plans). It was passed in response to corporate pension failures in the 1960s that left workers without promised benefits.</p>
<p><strong>Core concepts to internalize:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fiduciary duty.</strong> Anyone with discretion over plan assets or administration is a "fiduciary" and owes two specific duties:
  <ul>
  <li><strong>Duty of Loyalty</strong> — act solely in the interest of plan participants and beneficiaries.</li>
  <li><strong>Duty of Prudence</strong> — act with the care, skill, and diligence of a "prudent expert" in similar circumstances.</li>
  </ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Fiduciary roles</strong> (the alphabet soup):
  <ul>
  <li><strong>§3(16) — Plan Administrator.</strong> Handles plan administration (Form 5500, distributions, notices). Usually a TPA or the plan sponsor.</li>
  <li><strong>§3(21) — Investment Advice Fiduciary.</strong> Recommends investments. Non-discretionary. The plan committee decides.</li>
  <li><strong>§3(38) — Investment Manager.</strong> Has discretionary authority over investments. Takes on legal liability.</li>
  </ul>
</li>
<li><strong>§408(b)(2) — Service Provider Fee Disclosure.</strong> Service providers must disclose all direct and indirect compensation to plan fiduciaries. The transparency mechanism.</li>
<li><strong>§404(a) — Prudent Man Rule.</strong> General fiduciary standard.</li>
<li><strong>§404(c) — Participant-Directed Investments.</strong> If a plan lets participants choose their own investments and meets specific requirements, the fiduciary gets some liability protection for participants' choices.</li>
<li><strong>§406 — Prohibited Transactions.</strong> Specific transactions (self-dealing, related-party) flatly prohibited unless a Prohibited Transaction Exemption (PTE) applies.</li>
<li><strong>Form 5500.</strong> Annual filing with DOL/IRS disclosing plan financial info. Large plans (100+ eligible participants) require an independent audit.</li>
<li><strong>DOL</strong> is the primary regulator. The <strong>Employee Benefits Security Administration (EBSA)</strong> is the DOL sub-agency.</li>
</ul>
<p>For HUB: every RPW engagement is structured around these fiduciary roles. The advisory contract specifies whether HUB is a 3(21) or 3(38). The 408(b)(2) fee disclosures HUB provides are how plan sponsors meet their compliance obligation to know what they're paying.</p>
"""),
    ("SECURE Act (December 2019)",
     "First major retirement reform in over a decade",
     """
<p>Key provisions:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pooled Employer Plans (PEPs)</strong> — unrelated employers can join a single 401(k) sponsored by a <strong>Pooled Plan Provider (PPP)</strong>. The PPP handles most fiduciary and administrative duties. Goal: make 401(k) plans accessible to small employers.</li>
<li><strong>Multiple Employer Plans (MEPs)</strong> rules clarified.</li>
<li><strong>RMD age</strong> raised from 70½ to 72.</li>
<li><strong>Long-term part-time employees</strong> must be covered (3 consecutive years, 500+ hours).</li>
<li><strong>Lifetime income disclosures</strong> on participant statements.</li>
<li><strong>Safe harbor for annuity selection</strong> in DC plans.</li>
<li><strong>"One Bad Apple"</strong> rule removed — historically one bad employer could disqualify a whole MEP; now removed.</li>
</ul>
"""),
    ("SECURE 2.0 (December 2022)",
     "The more important one — 90+ provisions, phased 2023–2027",
     """
<p><strong>Effective 2023:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>RMD age raised again to 73. Rises to 75 by 2033.</li>
<li>Startup tax credit for new plans increased from 50% to 100% of costs (≤50 employees, $5K/year cap, 3 years).</li>
<li>New $1,000-per-employee credit for employer contributions to new plans.</li>
<li>403(b) plans can join PEPs/MEPs.</li>
<li>Inadvertent overpayment relief for plan fiduciaries.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Effective 2024:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Emergency Savings Accounts (PLSA — Pension-Linked Emergency Savings Account). Up to $2,500 inside the 401(k).</li>
<li>Student loan matching — employers can match student loan payments as 401(k) contributions.</li>
<li>Roth employer matching allowed (previously match had to be pre-tax).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Effective January 1, 2025 — the big one:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mandatory automatic enrollment</strong> for new 401(k) and 403(b) plans (established after Dec 29, 2022). Default contribution starts at 3%, escalates 1%/year to 10–15%. Existing plans grandfathered. Very small employers (&lt;10) and new businesses (&lt;3 years) exempt.</li>
<li><strong>Starter 401(k)</strong> plans for employers without existing plans — simplified plan type.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Effective 2026:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Roth catch-up requirement</strong> for high earners. If you earn over $145K (indexed), age-50+ catch-up contributions MUST be Roth (after-tax). Originally scheduled 2024; IRS delayed to 2026 because recordkeepers couldn't get systems ready. <strong>Forces all clients onto Roth-capable recordkeepers</strong> — drives RFP work.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Effective 2027:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Saver's Match replaces Saver's Credit — federal matching contribution directly to low-income workers' retirement accounts.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Why SECURE 2.0 matters strategically for HUB:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Auto-enrollment mandate creates a wave of new small-plan formation — HUB Retirement Select is positioned to capture this.</li>
<li>Roth catch-up requirement forces recordkeeper upgrades — HUB advises clients through transitions and runs the resulting RFPs.</li>
<li>Plan amendment deadline (most plans must be amended for SECURE 2.0 by <strong>December 31, 2026</strong>) creates billable consulting work right now.</li>
</ul>
"""),
    ("DOL Fiduciary Rule saga",
     "Still unresolved in 2026",
     """
<p>A long-running regulatory drama about who counts as an "investment advice fiduciary":</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>1975 DOL Rule</strong> — defined fiduciary using a five-part test. Many advisors structured their businesses to avoid it.</li>
<li><strong>2016 Obama DOL Rule</strong> — dramatically expanded fiduciary status. <em>Vacated by 5th Circuit in 2018.</em></li>
<li><strong>2020 Trump DOL Rule</strong> — adopted <strong>PTE 2020-02</strong>, a more limited approach requiring fiduciary advisors to follow "Impartial Conduct Standards" for rollover advice. Still in effect.</li>
<li><strong>2024 Biden DOL "Retirement Security Rule"</strong> — would have expanded fiduciary status again, especially for rollover advice and annuity sales. <em>Partially blocked by federal courts in late 2024.</em></li>
<li><strong>2025–2026</strong> — Under the second Trump administration, DOL signaled it will not defend the 2024 rule. PTE 2020-02 remains the operative standard.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> rollover advice is a regulatory hot zone. When HUB advises a 401(k) participant to roll over to a HUB-managed IRA, it must navigate PTE 2020-02 carefully — document why the rollover is in the participant's interest, comply with the Impartial Conduct Standards. This is operational reality at every aggregator and a non-trivial reporting requirement.</p>
"""),
    ("ERISA litigation",
     "The threat that drives demand",
     """
<p>Since the mid-2000s, an entire plaintiff's bar has emerged suing plan sponsors over alleged ERISA fiduciary breaches — usually claiming excessive fees, imprudent investment options, or self-dealing.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tussey v. ABB (2007)</strong> started the modern wave.</li>
<li><strong>Tibble v. Edison</strong> went to the Supreme Court (2015) — established ongoing duty to monitor investments.</li>
<li>Class-action filings against plan sponsors hit 200+ per year by the early 2020s.</li>
<li>Plaintiffs increasingly name <strong>the plan's advisor</strong> as a co-defendant, especially when the advisor was a 3(38) fiduciary.</li>
</ul>
<p>This litigation pressure is the single biggest driver of demand for HUB RPW services. Sponsors hire RPAs specifically to (a) reduce litigation risk through documented fiduciary process, (b) shift some of that risk via 3(38) delegation, and (c) get expert witnesses ready if they get sued. <strong>The audit trail isn't a nice-to-have — it's the product.</strong></p>
"""),
    ("HSO implication",
     "What this regulatory landscape means for an Unite implementation",
     """
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-eyebrow">For implementation teams</div>
<p>Two non-obvious things:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Documented decision lineage is regulatory output, not a UX preference.</strong> If we touch RPW data, every fund replacement, every fee change, every recommendation needs a defensible "who decided, on what evidence, on what date" chain. ERP Unite's existing AH journal four-eye control and authorization framework are the right primitives — but the workflows around RPW decisions need their own track, not just adapted carrier-statement workflows.</li>
<li><strong>The Roth catch-up requirement (Jan 1, 2026) is a near-term wedge.</strong> Every plan with high earners has to comply, which forces recordkeeper conversations across HUB's entire RPW book. If we can position ERP Unite (or an IntegrationHub adapter) as the system that ingests post-Roth-conversion data feeds cleanly, that's a credible foot-in-the-door.</li>
</ol>
</div>
"""),
    ("Test yourself",
     "Five questions before moving on",
     """
<ol class="test-yourself">
<li>What's the difference between §3(21) and §3(38) ERISA fiduciary roles?</li>
<li>What changed for new plans on January 1, 2025?</li>
<li>When does the Roth catch-up requirement for high earners actually take effect, and why was the date delayed?</li>
<li>What's PTE 2020-02 and why does HUB care?</li>
<li>Why does ERISA litigation actually <em>help</em> HUB's business?</li>
</ol>
"""),
],

"06-outlook": [
    ("The thesis",
     "Where HUB and RPW go in the next 24 months",
     """
<p>HUB is positioned at the intersection of three powerful tailwinds — <strong>demographics, regulation, convergence</strong> — and is preparing to monetize that positioning via an IPO in 2026–2027. The biggest risks are integration drag, fee compression, recordkeeper consolidation pressure, and ERISA litigation.</p>
"""),
    ("Demographic tailwind",
     "Peak 65 — the wave that doesn't stop until 2030",
     """
<ul>
<li>About <strong>11,200 Americans turn 65 every day</strong> during the 2024–2027 window — over 4.1 million per year.</li>
<li>The 65+ working population grew from 9.9 million in early 2019 to <strong>11.1 million in early 2025</strong> — more older people are working <em>and</em> saving.</li>
<li>DC plan assets are projected to remain in <strong>net outflows until ~2030</strong> as boomers retire and draw down — meaning DC asset growth in this period is driven by market appreciation more than new contributions.</li>
</ul>
<p>What this means for HUB:</p>
<ul>
<li>More near-retirees → more rollover IRA opportunities → more wealth-management revenue.</li>
<li>More retirement-income-planning demand → drives advisory fees on annuities, decumulation strategies, lifetime income solutions.</li>
<li>Older workforces → more demand for health/welfare benefits, financial wellness, transition planning.</li>
</ul>
"""),
    ("Regulatory tailwind",
     "Three concrete revenue events in the next 24 months",
     """
<ul>
<li><strong>SECURE 2.0 auto-enrollment mandate</strong> (effective Jan 2025) drives new plan formation. HUB Retirement Select is positioned for this.</li>
<li><strong>SECURE 2.0 plan amendment deadline</strong> (Dec 31, 2026 for most plans) creates billable consulting work right now.</li>
<li><strong>Roth catch-up requirement (2026)</strong> forces recordkeeper transitions = more RFP work for HUB RPW.</li>
<li><strong>State-mandated retirement plans</strong> spreading (CalSavers, OregonSaves, Illinois Secure Choice, NJ Secure Choice, etc.) — pushing employers to either adopt the state plan or sponsor their own. Many choose to sponsor and need an advisor.</li>
</ul>
"""),
    ("Convergence tailwind",
     "Why HUB's structural shape is the answer",
     """
<p>As covered in <a href="hubintl-04-rpa-industry.html">Module 4</a>, the strategic theme of retirement-plus-wealth-plus-benefits convergence favors HUB because HUB owns all three. Firms that don't have this combination are increasingly disadvantaged and are acquisition targets. HUB will keep buying them.</p>
"""),
    ("The IPO path",
     "What to expect",
     """
<ul>
<li><strong>Operational readiness target:</strong> late 2026 or early 2027 per Cohen.</li>
<li><strong>Likely valuation at IPO:</strong> $30B+ (current private mark is $29B; public peers Brown &amp; Brown and Gallagher trade at higher EBITDA multiples).</li>
<li><strong>Probable exchange:</strong> NYSE.</li>
<li><strong>Investor base preparation:</strong> The May 2025 round was designed to bring in public-market investors (T. Rowe Price, hedge funds, sovereign wealth) so the cap table looks pre-public by IPO time.</li>
<li><strong>What an IPO unlocks:</strong>
  <ul>
  <li>Stock currency for larger acquisitions (HUB could go after a publicly traded peer).</li>
  <li>Liquidity for employees (~30% historically own equity).</li>
  <li>Liquidity for the PE sponsors (H&amp;F, Altas, LGP) to gradually exit.</li>
  <li>Higher valuation multiple thanks to public-market scarcity premium.</li>
  </ul>
</li>
</ul>
"""),
    ("M&A trajectory",
     "What to expect from the acquisition engine",
     """
<p>HUB has done 60–70 acquisitions per year for the past four years (61 in 2024, 65 in 2023, 68 in 2022). Expect:</p>
<ul>
<li>Continued aggressive RPW acquisitions — Wise Rhino Group will remain the M&amp;A advisor of choice on the seller side.</li>
<li>More wealth-only acquisitions to balance the institutional-heavy RPW book (Veripax, Prestige Wealth Partners, Creative Financial Strategies are recent examples).</li>
<li>Possible <strong>transformational acquisition post-IPO</strong>. With public-market currency, HUB could acquire a competitor like SageView, or target a public mid-cap broker.</li>
