<li><strong>Late 2026 / early 2027:</strong> S-1 filing if conditions are favorable. IPO targets $30B+.</li>
<li><strong>2027:</strong> If public, first earnings cycles. Pressure to show consistent organic growth. M&amp;A may slow as integration becomes the public-market narrative.</li>
<li><strong>2027–2028:</strong> Possible large-scale acquisition once stock currency is available. Capture of a sizable RPA aggregator or EB-focused competitor.</li>
</ul>
"""),
    ("HSO implication",
     "The deal posture this points to",
     """
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-eyebrow">Engagement posture summary</div>
<ol>
<li><strong>Lead with integration.</strong> HUB's #1 named risk is integration drag across 600+ acquired entities. ERP Unite is literally a solution to that risk. Position it that way, not as "a better commission system."</li>
<li><strong>Audit-trail every conversation.</strong> Pre-IPO finance organizations care about SOX, defensible documentation, and reproducibility. Every demo should foreground these capabilities.</li>
<li><strong>RPW is the deal-defining question.</strong> If RPW is in scope, this is a multi-year product investment for HSO with material AUM-billing system extensions. If RPW is out of scope, this is a focused P&amp;C + EB engagement with IntegrationHub as the seam. Decide before discovery, don't drift.</li>
<li><strong>Mid-Atlantic is the easy first foothold.</strong> The AFS team (Bethesda, MD) + original Washington Financial Group team (McLean, VA) is geographically nearby, well-led, and culturally aligned with consulting partnerships. If a pilot region is on the table, this is it.</li>
