    ("section section-color-2", None, """
# The five plan types

A school district has a 403(b) + a 457(b). A tech company has a 401(k) + an NQDC. These come up in scope conversations.
"""),
    ("", "Plan type quick-reference", """
| Plan | Who can offer it | Key features |
|---|---|---|
| **401(k)** | Private employers | Most common DC; Roth + pre-tax; matched contributions |
| **403(b)** | Schools, 501(c)(3), religious | Similar to 401(k); historically annuity-heavy; some ERISA-exempt |
| **457(b)** | Government + top-hat | **No 10% early-withdrawal penalty**; can stack with 401(k)/403(b) |
| **Defined Benefit** | Private + public | Traditional pension; employer bears risk; mostly frozen now |
| **NQDC** | Top execs only | Above qualified plan limits; **unfunded** promise to pay |

*Net: TCG (HUB acquisition 2021, Texas) specialized in 403(b)/457(b). That deal was specifically how HUB entered the public-sector retirement market.*
"""),
    ("section section-color-3", None, """
# 3(21) vs 3(38)

The fiduciary distinction that comes up in every RPW conversation.
"""),
    ("", "Two fiduciary roles, very different liability", """
<div class="cols two">
<div class="card">

### **ERISA §3(21)**
**Investment Advice Fiduciary.**
- Advisor *recommends*; sponsor *decides*
- Sponsor bears the legal liability
- Lower fee, more common
- Committee still has to vote on every fund change

</div>
<div class="card">

### **ERISA §3(38)**
**Investment Manager.**
- Advisor has **discretionary authority**
- **Sponsor delegates fiduciary liability** for investment decisions
- Higher fee, less common
- The sponsor monitors the advisor; the advisor monitors the funds

</div>
</div>

*Net: larger plans increasingly prefer 3(38) because it offloads litigation risk. The industry trend is toward more 3(38) engagements — and higher fees with it.*
"""),
